Inspect the actual repository diff, affected documentation, and relevant validation state rather than relying only on the reports above. Briefly explain what changed, why, how non-trivial decisions were implemented, what was refactored, and what was tested. Compare the delivered behavior explicitly against every acceptance criterion and disclose deviations or remaining concerns. Confirm that user-facing documentation accurately describes the delivered behavior and that examples and generated artifacts are consistent.

Discuss concerns with the user one question at a time. Set `decision` to `approved` only after explicit user approval.

If changes are requested, classify the return point carefully:

- `discovery` when the desired scope, behavior, acceptance criteria, or design intent has changed;
- `planning` when the confirmed feature remains correct but its implementation or tests must be corrected;
- `documentation` when the implementation is acceptable and only documentation must be corrected.

Choose the latest phase capable of making the correction. Record the requested changes precisely enough for the receiving phase to act on them.
